prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages a continuous integration and deployment pipeline for a software company.

The people involved are committers, approvers, release managers, and site reliability engineers.

One person may hold both the committer and approver roles at the same time, and one person may hold both the approver and release manager roles at the same time.

The resources are deployments and artifacts.

Every deployment records its committer, its approver, its target environment, its change risk level, its artifact, and its status.

Every artifact records who built it, whether it has been signed, its build tags, and its status.

Each request to sign an artifact carries the current time, the source network address it was sent from, and whether it is an emergency change.

The committer who built an artifact must be the one who signs it.

A committer who did not build the artifact must not sign it, even if they are a committer, an approver, or a release manager on the same team.

An artifact may be signed only after its build has succeeded and its status shows as built.

An artifact may be signed only when it carries the "production-release" build tag.

An artifact may be signed only when the signing request comes from the corporate VPN network range.

A request by the committer who built a "production-release" artifact, whose build has succeeded, sent from the corporate VPN, to sign that artifact must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["committer", "approver", "releaseManager", "siteReliabilityEngineer"];

entity Team;

entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    team: Team,
};

entity Artifact {
    builtBy: User,
    hasBeenSigned: Bool,
    buildTags: Set<String>,
    status: String,
    buildSucceeded: Bool,
};

entity Deployment {
    committer: User,
    approver: User,
    targetEnvironment: String,
    changeRiskLevel: String,
    artifact: Artifact,
    status: String,
};

action signArtifact appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Artifact],
    context: {
        currentTime: datetime,
        sourceNetworkAddress: ipaddr,
        isEmergencyChange: Bool,
        sourceNetworkIsCorporateVpn: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
